  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "${\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2y}^{y+1}2xy\\,dx\\,dy=$",
  "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2y}^{y+1}2xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}y[{\\int }_{2y}^{y+1}2x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}y[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]_{2y}^{y+1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}y((y+1)^{2}-(2y)^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}y({y}^{2}+2y+1-4{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-3{y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}+y)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-3{y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}+y)\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{4}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{11}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
